## Which parser fixes are certain (fix.auto.1, ADR 1130). A parser diagnostic
## carries one fix, so certainty is a property of its rule: every fix these rules
## make rewrites another language's spelling into the one AIL form that means the
## same (`else if` as `elif`, `&&` as `and`, `??` as `else`, `let x =` as `x :=`).
## The checker marks its certain fixes one by one (`Diag.with_sure_fix`).
## Left out on purpose: `lex.kw.1` (it picks a new name), the string-escape rules
## and every fix that adds code the author didn't write (`todo` arms).
SURE_SYNTAX_RULES := [
    "gram.foreign.1",
    "gram.fn.2",
    "expr.op.1",
    "expr.bind.1",
    "expr.bind.2",
    "expr.bind.3",
    "expr.if.1",
    "expr.loop.8",
    "expr.match.1",
    "err.type.1",
    "mod.const.1",
    "mod.use.4",
    "type.coll.4",
    "type.distinct.1",
]

## Whether the parser's fix for `rule` is certain.
pub fn sure_syntax(rule str) bool
    SURE_SYNTAX_RULES.contains(rule)
